    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the reliance on perpetual economic growth is indeed a major critique, the mainstream argument is that decoupling full social democracy from high growth is both difficult and potentially detrimental to average workers, who would otherwise see wages and services fund their social needs.\n\nMany political economists disagree that the entire model is unjustifiable, instead arguing that current challenges represent a distributional issue where capital and labor must be better balanced rather than abandoning the social contract. They suggest that within a framework of \"degrowth\" or anti-liberal socialism, the current debate over growth might be unproductive or even counter to the goal of immediate welfare.\n\nThe core logical defense lies in the fact that for many voters, an increase in the guaranteed standard of living for the majority currently feels like a net benefit, even if it requires a higher overall \"cost\" to the system.",
